<main id="main">
<section class="phero"><div class="phero-bg" data-parallax="0.15"><img src="/assets/img/photos/apartment-complex.webp" srcset="/assets/img/photos/apartment-complex-640.webp 640w, /assets/img/photos/apartment-complex.webp 1024w" sizes="100vw" width="1024" height="682" alt="" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"></div><div class="wrap phero-in"><nav class="crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><ol><li><a href="/">Home</a></li><li><a href="/our-appraisal-services/">Services</a></li><li aria-current="page">Commercial &amp; HOA Appraisal</li></ol></nav><p class="kicker">Appraisal service</p><h1>Appraisal for Associations, Churches and Business Property</h1><p class="lede">Large buildings carry large claims, and a small percentage gap on a master policy can mean a lot of money. We value commercial losses so boards and owners can make decisions on solid numbers.</p><div class="btns"><a class="btn btn-gold" href="tel:+18445371427"><svg class="ico" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 16.9v3a2 2 0 0 1-2.2 2 19.8 19.8 0 0 1-8.6-3.1 19.5 19.5 0 0 1-6-6A19.8 19.8 0 0 1 2.1 4.2 2 2 0 0 1 4.1 2h3a2 2 0 0 1 2 1.7c.1.9.4 1.8.7 2.7a2 2 0 0 1-.5 2.1L8 9.8a16 16 0 0 0 6 6l1.3-1.3a2 2 0 0 1 2.1-.4c.9.3 1.8.6 2.7.7a2 2 0 0 1 1.7 2z"/></svg> Call (844) 537-1427</a><a class="btn btn-ghost" href="/talk-to-an-appraiser/">Free claim review</a></div><ul class="hero-facts"><li><small>Clients</small><b>HOAs, condos, churches, owners</b></li><li><small>Policies</small><b>Master and commercial forms</b></li><li><small>Excluded</small><b>Coverage and liability questions</b></li></ul></div></section><div class="wrap cols"><article class="prose"><h2>Who holds the claim on a master policy</h2>
<p>On a condominium or townhome property, the association’s master policy usually covers the buildings and common elements, while each owner’s individual policy covers interiors and personal property. Where that line falls depends on the governing documents and the policy. Before an appraisal starts, we sort out which parts of the loss belong to the association and which belong to unit owners, so nobody pays twice or falls through a gap.</p>
<p>Boards also need clear communication. We can give the board a written scope and estimate it can share with members, and we talk with the property manager about access to units, roofs and mechanical rooms.</p>
<h2>Buildings we price across the state</h2>
<ul>
<li>Condominium towers and townhome rows on the Coast, from Bay St. Louis to Ocean Springs.</li>
<li>Churches with steep roofs, steeples, stained glass, sanctuaries and fellowship halls.</li>
<li>Student rental complexes in Oxford and Starkville.</li>
<li>Strip centers, offices and medical buildings in Metro Jackson, Tupelo and Hattiesburg.</li>
<li>Metal farm and shop buildings in the Delta.</li>
<li>Hotels and hospitality properties along the Coast.</li>
</ul>
<p>Each type brings its own disputes. Church roofs often need steep and high charges, specialty flashing and crane time that a generic estimate misses. Multi-building complexes raise questions about uniform appearance across buildings, especially when only some roofs were replaced. Coastal properties bring named-storm deductibles, which the <a href="/claim-calculator-suite/named-storm-deductible-calculator/">named-storm deductible calculator</a> can help you estimate.</p>
<h2>Coinsurance can change the math</h2>
<p>Many commercial policies include a coinsurance clause. If the building was insured for less than a set percentage of its value at the time of loss, the payment can be reduced in proportion. That makes the building’s value — not just the repair cost — part of the dispute.</p>
<p><em>Illustrative example:</em> a policy with an 80% coinsurance clause on a building valued at $2,000,000 expects at least $1,600,000 of insurance. If the limit was $1,200,000, a carrier may argue the payment should be cut to three-quarters of the loss. An accurate replacement-cost valuation can matter as much as the repair estimate itself.</p>
<div class="callout"><b class="callout-t">Value, not liability</b><p>Appraisal can set the amount of a building loss, the replacement value and, where the clause allows, the amount of business income loss. It doesn’t decide coverage, whether an exclusion applies or whether the carrier owes extra damages. Those questions belong with a Mississippi attorney.</p></div>
<h2>Business income and extra expense</h2>
<p>When a covered loss shuts down a business, many commercial policies pay for lost income and extra expense during the period of restoration. The arguments are usually about the numbers: how long repairs should reasonably take, what the business would have earned, and which costs continued. Appraisal can often value that loss when the policy’s clause reaches it. Whether the income loss is covered in the first place stays a coverage question.</p>
<h3>What we ask commercial clients for</h3>
<p>Expect to share the full policy with schedules, building valuations or appraisals done for underwriting, maintenance and roof records, the carrier’s estimate and any engineer reports. For income claims, financial statements for the periods before and after the loss help.</p>
<h2>How a commercial file moves</h2>
<p>The steps match any appraisal — written demand, appointment of appraisers, umpire selection and a two-of-three award — but commercial files run on more documents and more people. The <a href="/appraisal-roadmap/">appraisal roadmap</a> lays out the stages. For Coast associations facing hurricane losses, our <a href="/our-appraisal-services/hurricane-named-storm-appraisal/">hurricane appraisal</a> page explains how MWUA wind coverage and flood fit together, and the <a href="/counties-and-cities/biloxi-harrison-county/">Biloxi and Harrison County</a> page covers local conditions.</p>
<h2>Available across Mississippi</h2>
<p>Commercial &amp; HOA Appraisal is available statewide. Inspections are scheduled around your timeline in places such as <a href="/counties-and-cities/columbus-lowndes-county/">Columbus</a>, <a href="/counties-and-cities/greenville-washington-county/">Greenville</a>, <a href="/counties-and-cities/jackson-hinds-county/">Jackson</a>, <a href="/counties-and-cities/gulfport-harrison-county/">Gulfport</a>.</p>
<h2>Commercial &amp; HOA Appraisal questions</h2><div class="faqs"><details><summary>Can an HOA board demand appraisal without a member vote?</summary><p>That depends on your governing documents and Mississippi law, not on the policy alone. Many boards act through their officers, but confirm your authority with the association’s attorney.</p></details><details><summary>Can appraisal decide our business income loss?</summary><p>Often it can value the amount of the income loss when the policy’s clause reaches it. Whether the income loss is covered remains a separate question.</p></details><details><summary>Do churches use the same appraisal process?</summary><p>Yes. A church’s property policy usually has the same kind of appraisal clause, though steep roofs, steeples and stained glass make the estimate more involved.</p></details></div>
